#: 2b6a7e9043f240c69671e3651f38e29f
msgid "Delete the contents of *ArduPilot\\\\Logs* directory and, assuming you are in the *ArduPilot* directory in Cygwin, run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:133
#: f1538f9eac51418ebbedde614d197072
msgid "Run *waf-light distclean* and erase the *ArduPilot\\\\Logs* directory every time you make a build that you actually intend to use (as opposed to building just for the sake of checking whether your new code compiles and links without errors). Building the ArduPlane binaries with waf-light from scratch, as above, takes only a few minutes. Not doing full cleanup beforehand occasionally results in extremely bizarre behavior of the produced binaries that can take much more time to investigate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:138
#: ead5e9c014e844139eed071286524b82
msgid "Running soaring SITL with X-Plane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:140
#: 0d9a57b2a02843ba999f0d3437bc492c
msgid "**Launch ArduPlane SITL** by running the following from the *ArduPilot* directory in Cygwin:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:146
#: 12d5b337792744b889651da4f600fab0
msgid "Or by launching SITL from MissionPlanner's Simulation screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:150
#: f7d50114ffca4347a642be9b1781cf4c
msgid "**Launch X-Plane, choose an RC sailplane, and choose the environmental conditions.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:152
#: e184453b22bd4c73adfa6d190a1f5717
msgid "Recall that in step 3 of `Setting up X-Plane <https://ardupilot.org/dev/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.html#setting-up-x-plane>`_, you put your RC sailplane models into *%XPlane-install-dir%\\\\Aircraft\\\\RC Sailplanes*, so look for them there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:155
#: 90ce23f0848c4496b4accef55ebdc739
msgid "Start with the Vee-gull 005. It has a motor, making it easier to fly long missions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:158
#: 5459c4d0c03d47ca9e3fcf02e793d873
msgid "Since RC sailplanes are much lighter than full-sized aircraft, they are much more affected by weather phenomena. X-Plane models this, to the point that your an RC sailplane can get flipped by a light wind gust *while sitting on the ground*! To avoid frustration, set environmental conditions in X-Plane to be calm and clear. See the selections in the screenshot below for an example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:165
#: 516546532a6b41909b82ee6c14d24982
msgid "**Launch Mission Planner and connect it to SITL.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:167
#: 277ba318b0ab4d0b962e9a0b787dfb0b
msgid "Mission Planner and SITL will talk via the TCP protocol. To establish the connection, choose “TCP” from the drop-down menu near the *Connect* button in the top right corner of the Mission Planner window, hit the *Connect* button, and specify port 5760:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:173
#: dd5e8ab3252148bba905332d1b7167f8
msgid "The first time you establish a connection between Mission Planner and ArduPlane SITL after building ArduPlane, before you load ArduPlane parameters as described in the next step, your X-Plane RC plane (which is presumably on the ground when the connection is established) may flip over and crash or otherwise behave strangely. This is normal and won’t happen after you load a *.param* file for it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:177
#: 4b9e186bd4af4992a147298b1498de05
msgid "**Set** `ArduPilot's parameters <https://ardupilot.org/copter/docs/parameters.html>`_ **for the chosen RC sailplane.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:179
#: efbaa31f6bc441fe815cc7b59b951df5
msgid "*This step needs to be done only the first time you run SITL after (re-)building ArduPlane.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:181
#: 10595257d5c24a14943ebd2a0c00a586
msgid "The parameters can be set via Mission Planner either individually or by loading a *.param* file for your aircraft, if you have one available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:183
#: b34e27628b3c4875b5f9626a4969da54
msgid "For the Vee-gull 005 model, the `vee-gull 005.param file is in ArduPilot\\\\Tools\\\\autotest\\\\default_params <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/tree/master/Tools/autotest/default_params>`_. To use it, in Mission Planner, go to the *CONFIG/TUNING* screen, *Full Parameter List* tab, and then (1) press **Load from file** to load the file and (2) press **Write Params** to actually write the parameters to ArduPlane's memory, as shown on the following screenshot:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:188
#: 83f92f39068c420ca21fcc8ce27aca64
msgid "When you load and set parameters from a file for the first time after (re-)building ArduPlane, Mission Planner may display a message such as \"Parameter refresh required --- press F5\", and the simulation may even crash -- you will see \"ERROR: Floating point exception - aborting\" in the Cygwin window where you started the simulation. This is not unusual. Just restart the simulation by running ``build/sitl/bin/arduplane --model xplane`` in Cygwin, reconnect to it by pressing *Connect* in Mission Planner, and repeat the above process for loading parameters from a file. This time everything will work smoothly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:191
#: a04969df7b754dd2a85195348395f8b8
msgid "ArduPilot's parameters largely determine how an aircraft behaves, and setting them correctly is very important. This is especially true for :ref:`soaring-specific <soaring>` parameters. Failure to choose appropriate values for them can cause a sailplane to miss all thermals or repeatedly try to catch a thermal where there is none. Refer to `the instructions for calculating these parameters <https://ardupilot.org/plane/docs/soaring.html#tune-the-tecs>`_ for your sailplane model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:194
#: 7919624f39ae422b80296db196ced91c
msgid "If everything has worked correctly so far, you should see the Cygwin window where you launched SITL start getting filled with notifications of the kind ``Data rate: 19.9 FPS  Frame rate:466.9 FPS``, like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:201
#: 4b974d95f5f7439c93f690824e077c8a
msgid "**Activate thermals in X-Plane.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:203
#: ea50e8a4fe5a475d83fd2d6d55a00879
msgid "Go to the *Environment* menu in the menu bar at the top of the X-Plane window (if necessary, mouse over near the top of the window to make it appear) and choose *Weather*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:208
#: 93ace0ab936f46d9a2673a1bbb0b76d4
msgid "To enable thermals, set *thermal coverage* to something higher than 0 and possibly change *thermal climb-rate*. It should be higher than you sailplane's `SOAR_VSPEED <https://ardupilot.org/plane/docs/soaring.html#set-up-the-soaring-parameters>`_ parameter. To make the mission a bit more challenging, you can also set wind speed, gust, shear, and turbulence above 0, but don't overdo it --- setting turbulence higher than 1 will make an RC sailplane-sized aircraft very difficult to control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:210
#: 8d60d528d33847e489775715a9ed7c30
msgid "The settings in the screenshot below are an example. The only changes from the defaults are in the thermal settings and low-altitude wind layer:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:216
#: b896fa2064b8464191847e6533f18415
msgid "You must re-activate thermals and any other weather settings every time you restart the simulation in X-Plane, e.g., by crashing your plane!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:219
#: bb495600caa44c2684eee2f920c3a446
msgid "If your aircraft does not seem to be detecting any thermals, the first thing to check is whether thermals are enabled in X-Plane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:223
#: e87eb857e3c049a2aa5f960f5aba7a0a
msgid "**Load a mission and fly!**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:225
#: 4dd52fa2e2f0400c82b4088992184773
msgid "To set your RC sailplane on a mission, load a mission file by going to Mission Planner's *FLIGHT PLAN* screen, (1) clicking **Load WP file** to read in a mission, and (2) clicking **Write WPs** to send the mission specification to your virtual RC sailplane, as shown below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:230
#: 6db9a467ae1f40328fcc32c93db09b14
msgid "An example `Soaring in Seattle.waypoints <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/tree/master/Tools/autotest/XPlane>`_ mission file is available in *ArduPlane\\\\Tools\\\\autotest\\\\X-Plane*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:232
#: c48d3b2061e546afab68a0bf0ee8f700
msgid "To fly a mission, have your RC sailplane take off in X-Plane, and then in Mission Planner set the mode to *Auto*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:238
#: 3cebbb91d472413f9e4701528d4bb262
msgid "Vee-gull 005 can be tricky to get into the air in the conventional way. It sits very low on the ground, and giving it a lot of throttle sharply, especially before releasing the brakes, will cause it to strike the prop against the ground. This may not have any immediate effects, but X-Plane's damage model will make the engine lose effectiveness eventually, seemingly for no apparent reason."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:240
#: 500c1427c31247bdb429ce274130213e
msgid "Open the throttle very gently when taking off under power in a Vee-gull 005, and leave the ground just as soon as you gain enough speed!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/soaring-sitl-with-xplane.rst:243
#: d6c2ee75ed8f4f9f8b8873cd0bd58c3e
msgid "To avoid the aforementioned risk of prop strike at takeoff when using Vee-gull 005, take off with a glider winch instead. To do so, in X-Plane go to the *Aircraft* menu and choose *Aircraft & Situations*:"
msgstr ""

